# Code that runs

A fence marked `run` is shown as code and is also a module the page imports. The component that [replaces `pre`](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-block-components) gets that module as a prop and decides what running means: render a component, call an export, print a result.

::::plate[A real module](mark="run", tone=fence)
The block is a real module in the Vite graph, not a string that is evaluated:

````svx score
```svelte run
<script>
	let count = $state(0);
</script>

<button onclick={() => count++}>clicked {count}</button>
```
````

:::notes
TypeScript is compiled, a Svelte block is compiled by vite-plugin-svelte, and its styles are scoped as in any component. Imports in the block resolve as they would in the document, so `./Button.svelte` is a file beside it, and aliases and `#lib/…` work. The server renders it with the page, and a content security policy has nothing to object to.

On the atlas: [code fence](/atlas#code-fence).
:::

:::edges
- A block can't see the document's `<script>`. It is a module of its own, as a file would be, so share values by importing them in both, or let your component pass props to what the block exports.
:::
::::

See the [code that runs playground example](/playground/run).

## A component for `pre`

::::plate[A `pre` that runs blocks](mark="<pre>", tone=comp)
mdsvex hands over the module and nothing else, so a document with a block that runs needs a `pre` replacement, from a [`components` module](/docs/guides/components#the-components-option) or a [template](/docs/guides/components#replacing-elements-in-a-template). This one renders a Svelte block as a component and calls the default export of a script block, each above its code:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Pre.svelte -->
<script>
	let { lang, module, load, children } = $props();

	let loaded = $state();
	const ran = $derived(module ?? loaded);
	const Demo = $derived(lang === 'svelte' ? ran?.default : undefined);
	const result = $derived(
		lang !== 'svelte' && typeof ran?.default === 'function'
			? ran.default()
			: undefined
	);
</script>

{#if Demo}
	<div class="demo"><Demo /></div>
{:else if result !== undefined}
	<output class="demo">{String(result)}</output>
{:else if load}
	<button onclick={async () => (loaded = await load())}>run</button>
{/if}
{@render children()}
```

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as pre } from './markdown/Pre.svelte';
```

````svx score
```ts run
import { format } from './dates';

export default () => format(new Date(2026, 0, 1));
```
````

:::notes
`module` is the namespace of the block, so `module.default` is its default export and its named exports are beside it. A block without the flag gets no `module`, so the same component serves every fence.
:::

:::edges
- Without a `pre` replacement the build fails at the fence.
:::
::::

::::plate[A frame around a demo](mark=":::", tone=directive)
Layout around a demo is a job for a [directive](/docs/guides/directives): the `pre` inside it renders the demo, and the directive's component puts a frame, a title or tabs around it.

````svx score
:::Example[A counter]

```svelte run
<button>…</button>
```

:::
````
::::

## Languages that run

::::plate[Languages that run](mark="ts", tone=fence)
`js`, `ts`, `jsx`, `tsx` and `svelte` blocks run, as do `javascript` and `typescript`. The language picks the extension of the module, which is how Vite knows what to do with it.

:::edges
- `run` on a block in any other language, or on one with no language, fails the build:

````svx error
```py run
print(1)
```
````
:::
::::

## Running on the client

::::plate[`run=client`](mark="client", tone=fence)
A block that touches `window` when it loads can't render on the server, and a heavy demo below the fold is better fetched late. `run=client` leaves the module out of the page and hands the component a function that imports it:

````svx score
```js run=client
export default () => window.innerWidth;
```
````

:::notes
The component gets `load` in place of `module`. Call it from an event handler or an effect, and it resolves to the same namespace `module` would hold. The block becomes a chunk of its own in a build.
:::
::::

## Running a file

::::plate[`src=` with `run`](mark="src=", tone=fence)
With [`src=`](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-from-a-file) a block shows a file, and with `run` as well the page imports that file. The component gets `src` and `module` both.

````svx score
```svelte src=./Counter.svelte run
```
````

:::notes
The module is the whole file, also when a slice shows only some of its lines.
:::
::::

## Editing a block

The id of a block's module is a hash of its language and code. In dev, an edit to one block changes only that block's import, so the page updates and every other block keeps the module it has: its top-level code doesn't run again and nothing it fetched is fetched again. Two blocks with the same code share one module.

An error in a block, at build time or in the browser, points at its line of the document.

## When a block can't run

::::plate[When a block can't run](mark="run=?", tone=fence)
These fail the build, each at the fence:

- no component replaces `pre`;
- the language is not one that runs;
- the block is also [live code](/docs/reference/live-code), with the `eval` flag or an `[!eval]` marker, since braces mean different things in each;
- `run=` has a value other than `client`.

:::edges
- A block that is live and runs:

````svx error
```js run eval
export default {a}
```
````

- A value other than `client`:

````svx error
```js run=server
export default 1
```
````
:::
::::

## Running with `compile`

::::plate[`snippets`](mark="snip", tone=expr)
`compile()` emits the import, but it has no bundler to serve the module from, so by default a block that runs throws a `RunError`. Say how the block is imported with `snippets`:

```js
const { code, snippets } = compile(source, {
	filename: 'guide.md',
	snippets: true
});
```

:::notes
`true` imports each block as a sibling of `filename`, `./guide.md.snippet-<hash>.<extension>`, the id the Vite plugin uses. A function gets the snippet and returns the specifier. Either way the result has `snippets`, one for each module, with its `hash`, `lang`, `extension`, `code` and the `line` of the document the code starts on, for your bundler's resolve and load hooks to serve. `snippets: false` runs no block: the flag is taken out of the meta and the fence is only shown.

A block with `src=` imports its file, so it needs no `snippets`.
:::

:::edges
- A `RunError` has a `code` (`run_module`, `run_pre`, `run_lang`, `run_eval` or `run_value`) and a `line` and `column`:

````svx error
```svelte run
<button>…</button>
```
````
:::
::::

The editor shows a block that runs as code, and reports one the build would reject at its fence. It doesn't check the code as a module yet.
